{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat memdiag-reader-brief-claims.md memdiag-packet-claims-2.json'","aggregated_output":"# Gate 2 claims reading (read-only)\n\nYou are an independent reader. Read only this brief and the packet named in your instructions. Do not open other files or run commands or model calls.\n\nEach entry is one answer of a research step to one article. It lists the **claims** the answer must keep, **stored reference items**\n(how an earlier answer kept them), the **link rule**, the **open theses** the answer could link to, the answer's **new theses**, and its\n**kept evidence** items with the thesis ids each links to.\n\nFor each claim, decide whether the answer keeps it: a kept evidence item states the same figure or event for the same measure as the\nclaim (the item may carry more, and the facts may sit in one item). A detail of the claim (a speaker, a count of companies, a\npercentage) is required only where the stored reference items state it. Then decide whether it is linked as the link rule says.\nJudge by meaning, not by matching words. If `answer_usable` is false, every claim is not kept.\n\nReturn one JSON object per entry, one per line, in packet order, and nothing else:\n`{\"n\": 1, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"kept\": true|false, \"linked_as_required\": true|false, \"item\": \"<evidence id or null>\", \"why\": \"one sentence\"}]}`\n[\n {\n  \"n\": 28,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E2\",\n    \"summary\": \"In Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack is about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose from 5–10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million full-sourcing estimate, as procurement structure materially moves the final price.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E4\",\n    \"summary\": \"The MarketScale report cites constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market.\",\n     \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, roughly double the ~$4 million of the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume. The bank says the GPUs are not the driver of the increase.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Nvidia Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"In Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, memory rose from 5-10% of the GB200 rack cost to 25-30% of the Vera Rubin VR200 rack cost, while the GPU's share of the bill of materials fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"In the same analysis, Morgan Stanley Research indicates first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter. The item attributes the schedule to the bank's analysis, not to an Nvidia-confirmed date.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million from its ~$7.8 million estimate, and that procurement structure can move final rack pricing by $1 million or more per rack.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\",\n     \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"The item reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 29,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-1\",\n    \"question\": \"What will contract memory prices — DRAM/LPDDR5X, 3D NAND and high-bandwidth memory — be over calendar 2027, given constrained supply against AI data-center demand and memory's rise to 25–30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack bill of materials?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The level of contract DRAM (incl. LPDDR5X), NAND and HBM prices in calendar 2027 versus 2026, and whether the supply squeeze behind the memory cost increase persists or eases.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2027 — set by the reason: the item (dated 6 July 2026) describes the memory cost shift for the second half of 2026 and beyond and the constrained supply behind it, so the next full calendar year is 2027.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research, in a bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale, estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers about $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, with Nvidia charging about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume. Memory content is put at about $2 million per rack, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage. Memory's share of the bill of materials rises to 25–30% from 5–10% while the GPU's share falls from about 63% to 51%, and Morgan Stanley notes a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"According to a Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials analysis, memory now accounts for roughly a quarter of the total, and the overall system price has nearly doubled generation over generation.\",\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\",\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\",\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research's analysis, as reported by MarketScale, has Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 racks scheduled for first shipments in the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. The AI buildout and the price of the memory in a laptop are now tied to the same supply squeeze.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 30,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and the bank estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; as a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Vera Rubin first shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, per the article's account of Morgan Stanley's analysis.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev4\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 31,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 AI rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers about $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, as reported by MarketScale.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\",\n     \"The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content in Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million per rack, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems, as reported by MarketScale.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"In Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale, memory's share of Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 rack rose to 25–30% from 5–10% on the GB200 generation, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"First shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin (VR200 NVL72) racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, per MarketScale's report of Morgan Stanley's analysis.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market.\",\n     \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-6\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes, as reported by MarketScale, that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 rack price down to around $6.7 million, showing procurement structure can materially move final pricing.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 32,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, and notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\",\n     \"The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage absent from earlier systems. Memory rose from 5 to 10 percent of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis of Nvidia's next-generation AI rack, first shipments of the Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory supply is constrained against AI-driven demand, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. It adds, citing Morgan Stanley's breakdown, that memory supply tightness and lead times should be treated as a scheduling risk for AI racks comparable to accelerator allocation.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n     \"Treat memory supply as a scheduling risk, not only a cost. With memory representing a quarter or more of rack value and supply tight, availability and lead times deserve the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 33,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E2\",\n    \"summary\": \"In Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack is about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose from 5–10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million full-sourcing estimate, as procurement structure materially moves the final price.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E4\",\n    \"summary\": \"The MarketScale report cites constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market.\",\n     \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\",\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage; memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5–10% on the GB200 to 25–30% on the VR200 while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to the Morgan Stanley analysis reported by MarketScale, NVIDIA's Vera Rubin first shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market.\",\n     \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 34,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"mem-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will contract memory prices (DRAM/DDR5, 3D NAND and HBM) rise over calendar 2026, given AI-driven demand against constrained supply?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The 2026 change in contract memory prices — the cost line that has become 25–30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack bill of materials and a strategic budget variable across the AI buildout.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2026 (Q4 2026 contract pricing) — set by the item, which reports contract DDR5 pricing has already 'climbed sharply' as of mid-2026 and that memory is 25–30% of the VR200 rack bill of materials for the second half of 2026 and beyond.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume; the bank notes a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\",\n     \"The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content in Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million per rack, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage; memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5–10% on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200 while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"mem-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"In Morgan Stanley's analysis as reported by MarketScale, first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass the higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market.\",\n     \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"mem-1\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 35,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E2\",\n    \"summary\": \"In Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack is about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose from 5–10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million full-sourcing estimate, as procurement structure materially moves the final price.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E4\",\n    \"summary\": \"The MarketScale report cites constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market.\",\n     \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"On a bill-of-materials basis, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent of the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent of the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent, per the Morgan Stanley analysis cited.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley projects that first shipments of the Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\",\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article attributes the rising rack cost to constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, notes contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and says device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, tying AI-buildout memory prices to the same supply squeeze.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. The AI buildout and the price of the memory in a laptop are now tied to the same supply squeeze.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\",\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-6\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 36,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\",\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\",\n     \"The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems. As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis states that Nvidia's Vera Rubin first shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"The item reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is behind rising component costs, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. It notes that with memory representing a quarter or more of rack value and supply tight, memory availability and lead times deserve the same scheduling attention as GPU allocation.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n     \"Treat memory supply as a scheduling risk, not only a cost. With memory representing a quarter or more of rack value and supply tight, availability and lead times deserve the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, indicating that procurement structure materially moves the final price.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 37,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E2\",\n    \"summary\": \"In Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack is about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose from 5–10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million full-sourcing estimate, as procurement structure materially moves the final price.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E4\",\n    \"summary\": \"The MarketScale report cites constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market.\",\n     \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-ai-memory-contract-price-2026\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will contract prices for AI-server memory — DDR5/LPDDR5X DRAM and 3D NAND used in NVIDIA's AI racks — rise in calendar 2026, given that memory is now a quarter-plus of rack cost and is flagged as constrained against AI-driven demand?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The calendar-2026 contract-price change for DDR5/LPDDR5X DRAM and 3D NAND, an input cost that bears on NVIDIA's rack pricing and product cost and on how far hyperscaler capex goes.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026, settled by Q4 2026 contract-price releases from TrendForce/DRAMeXchange (published early 2027); the item (published 2026-07-06) reports the increase is already under way but gives no figure or release date of its own.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1-vr200-rack-price\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research's bill-of-materials analysis, reported by MarketScale on 2026-07-06, estimates NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 AI rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation; the bank estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, and says the GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The Morgan Stanley report's own date is not given.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2-vr200-memory-content\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley, as reported by MarketScale on 2026-07-06, puts memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-ai-memory-contract-price-2026\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3-vr200-first-shipment-schedule\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reported on 2026-07-06 that first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter; the article does not attribute the schedule to NVIDIA or another named source.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\",\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4-vr200-bom-mix\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale, citing Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis on 2026-07-06, reports that memory rose from 5 to 10 percent of the GB200 rack's bill of materials to 25 to 30 percent on the Vera Rubin VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-ai-memory-contract-price-2026\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e5-memory-supply-ddr5-pricing\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reported on 2026-07-06 that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is the force behind rising AI rack prices and is raising component costs across the market; contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-ai-memory-contract-price-2026\",\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e6-procurement-moves-rack-price\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale, citing Morgan Stanley, reported on 2026-07-06 that procurement structure can move NVIDIA's final VR200 rack pricing by more than $1 million per rack, noting that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million from the roughly $7.8 million estimate.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\",\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-ai-memory-contract-price-2026\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 38,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\",\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"E1\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to Morgan Stanley Research estimates reported by Marketscale, Nvidia's Vera Rubin (VR200 NVL72) racks are scheduled for first shipments in the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter of 2026.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"E2\",\n    \"summary\": \"A Morgan Stanley Research bill-of-materials analysis estimates Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND. Memory rose from 5–10% of the bill of materials on the GB200 to 25–30% on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%; a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\",\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"E3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Marketscale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market; contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. The article flags memory supply constraints and tight availability as warranting the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\",\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 39,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-1\",\n    \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI-relevant memory (DDR5/DRAM, LPDDR5X, 3D NAND and HBM) do over calendar 2026, the input cost that now makes up roughly a quarter to 30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack bill of materials?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The direction and magnitude of 2026 memory contract pricing (DRAM/DDR5, LPDDR5X, 3D NAND, HBM) after the reported 435% jump in memory content per Nvidia AI rack, and whether memory inflation continues or eases.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2026, with emphasis on H2 2026 — set because the item frames AI infrastructure budgeting for the second half of 2026 and beyond, reports memory supply tight and contract DDR5 pricing climbing sharply, and gives no specific release date of its own.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research, in a bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale, estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, with Nvidia estimated to charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold rise in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; memory rose from 5-10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25-30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research expects first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack to be scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million from the roughly $7.8 million estimated otherwise, showing procurement structure materially moves the number.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 40,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\",\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-memory-prices\",\n    \"question\": \"Will contract prices for DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory keep rising through the second half of 2026, sustaining the roughly 435% jump in memory content per Nvidia Vera Rubin rack (about $2 million, 25–30% of the rack bill of materials) — the shared input cost that shapes Nvidia's rack pricing, hyperscaler AI budgets and memory availability for the VR200 ramp?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of memory (DRAM/NAND/HBM) contract prices in H2 2026, and whether memory content per Vera Rubin rack stays near $2 million with availability sufficient for the Q4 2026 volume ramp.\",\n    \"period\": \"Second half of 2026 (item dated 2026-07-06; the item says buyers must budget for the second half of 2026 and beyond), checked against Q3 and Q4 2026 memory contract-price releases.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1-vr200-rack-price\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume; the bank also notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2-first-shipments-schedule\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia's first shipments of the Vera Rubin platform are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, as reported in the article.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3-memory-content-bom\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5–10% on the GB200 to 25–30% on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-memory-prices\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4-memory-supply-pricing\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is the force behind the memory cost increase in Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack and the same force now raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 41,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\",\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, nearly double the roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, with memory now roughly a quarter of the total cost.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin rack will cost hyperscalers $7.8 million—nearly double the prior generation—with memory now accounting for 25-30% of total cost instead of 5-10%.\",\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\",\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform is scheduled for first shipments in the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 42,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\",\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, nearly double the roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and the bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; as a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"First shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"The item reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market; contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, and that procurement structure can move final pricing by more than $1 million per rack.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\",\n     \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 43,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\",\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research, in a bill-of-materials analysis, estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers about $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume. It also notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million, so procurement structure can move final pricing by $1+ million per rack.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that Nvidia's Vera Rubin (VR200) first shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates memory content in the Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a roughly threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems. Memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5–10% on the GB200 to 25–30% on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, tying the AI buildout's memory costs to the same supply squeeze.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. The AI buildout and the price of the memory in a laptop are now tied to the same supply squeeze.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 44,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\",\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \